{
  "schema": 1,
  "grade": "7",
  "band": "6-8",
  "focus": "Grade 7 is the year students learn that words carry attitude and that claims need support. Units move through tone and connotation, research and sources, systems, conflict and compromise, data, innovation, power and fairness, and probability, and the word-part work shifts to Latin roots whose meanings students must hold across prefixes (ced/cess, tract, vert/vers, fract/sect) plus the number prefixes uni-, mono-, bi-, tri- and multi-. Compared with grade 6 the words are more abstract and more evaluative; compared with grade 8 they stay at the level of identifying tone, claims and structure before students analyze bias, rhetoric and ethics.",
  "selection": "Words were chosen where a seventh grader's reading outruns writing and speaking: tone and attitude adjectives, research and claim-testing verbs, system and data terms, and conflict and fairness vocabulary. Priorities were academic utility across the disciplines (the public Academic Word List was used only as a check on utility, not for order or grouping), comprehension of informational text, shades of meaning, productive Latin morphology, Spanish cognates for multilingual learners, and confusable pairs (assume/presume, plausible/probable, partial/impartial, objective/subjective). Tier 3 terms appear only in disciplinary and literary units, at most three per disciplinary unit. Words and word parts already owned by grades 4 and 5 (the roots port, dict, struct, rupt, spect, scrib/script and the prefixes inter-, sub-, super-, trans-, anti-, post-) were not repeated. Bias, rhetoric, ethics, credibility and the roots cred, bene/mal and chron were deliberately held for grade 8, and the concrete character, observation and cause-effect vocabulary stays in grade 6. No publisher list or sequence was consulted.",
  "arc": [
    "What the Voice Is Saying — Tone and attitude: reading the feeling behind the words",
    "Digging for Answers — Research and inquiry: finding and checking sources",
    "One, Two, Many — Number prefixes uni-/mono-, bi-, tri- and multi-",
    "Command Words for Analysis — Task verbs and nouns for analytical writing",
    "Parts and Wholes — Systems: how components work together",
    "Claims That Hold Up — Argument: claims, reasoning and counterclaims",
    "Go, Yield, Step Back — The roots ced and cess: going, yielding and stopping",
    "Escalate or Defuse — Conflict and resolution: how disputes grow and end",
    "Reading the Numbers — Statistics and data: describing a set of numbers",
    "Voice and Viewpoint — Literary analysis: point of view and the reliability of a narrator",
    "Pull, Draw, Drag — The root tract: pulling and drawing",
    "Making a Living — Economics: work, money and exchange",
    "Who Holds Power — Civics and government: systems of authority",
    "Turn, Turn, Turn — The roots vert and vers: turning",
    "Persuasion Under the Surface — Persuasive techniques: how messages move people",
    "Courage Under Pressure — Resilience and endurance: character tested",
    "Break and Cut — The roots fract/frag and sect: breaking and cutting",
    "Poems: Forms and Feelings — Poetry: forms, sound and emotional effect",
    "Maybe, Probably, Possibly — Uncertainty and probability: degrees of knowing",
    "Fair or Not? — Fairness and justice: who gets treated how"
  ],
